BFS (Breadth-First Search) → explores level by level
DFS (Depth-First Search) → explores as deep as possible first

🚦 When to Use BFS
Use BFS when the problem is about shortest path or minimum steps.
  ✅ Signals for BFS
    “minimum steps”, “shortest distance”, “least moves”
    Level-wise traversal needed
    Unweighted graph
    Finding nearest/closest target
  💡 Examples
    Shortest path in a grid (like islands, maze)
    Word ladder problem
    Minimum moves in a game
    Level order traversal of a tree
  
🌳 When to Use DFS
Use DFS when you need to explore all possibilities or go deep
  ✅ Signals for DFS  
    “find all paths”
    “check if exists”
    Backtracking problems
    Tree/graph traversal without shortest path requirement
💡 Examples
    Detect cycle in graph
    Path existence
    Generate subsets / permutations
    Island counting (can use both, but DFS is simpler)
    Topological sort (DFS version)

START
  |
  |-- Is it asking for SHORTEST / MIN steps / nearest?
  |        → YES → ✅ BFS
  |
  |-- Is it level-by-level traversal?
  |        → YES → ✅ BFS
  |
  |-- Do you need to explore ALL paths / combinations?
  |        → YES → ✅ DFS
  |
  |-- Is it a TREE problem?
  |        |-- Level order → BFS
  |        |-- Depth / path / recursion → DFS
  |
  |-- Is it GRAPH cycle detection or topological sort?
  |        → DFS (most common)
  |
  |-- Grid problem?
           |-- Shortest path → BFS
           |-- Count islands / flood fill → DFS

🧩 Real Interview Patterns
  1. Grid Problems (Islands)
    Count islands → DFS (simple)
    Shortest path in grid → BFS
  2. Tree Problems
    Level order → BFS
    Depth / path sum → DFS
  3. Graph Problems
    Shortest path → BFS
    Cycle detection → DFS
